"Bollobás–Nikiforov conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/405423/bollobas-nikiforov-conjecture)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; This conjecture was proposed by Béla Bollobás and Vladimir Nikiforov in their 2007 paper Cliques and the spectral radius. ... Bollobás and Nikiforov proposed this spectral strengthening of Turán’s theorem in 2007, asserting an upper bound for the two largest adjacency eigenvalues of every non-complete graph.\n\n# Bollobás–Nikiforov conjecture\n\nLet $G$G be a finite simple graph with $m$m edges and clique number $\\omega(G)$ω(G). Let\n\n$$\\lambda_1(G)\\ge \\lambda_2(G)\\ge \\cdots \\ge \\lambda_n(G)$$λ 1​(G)≥λ 2​(G)≥⋯≥λ n​(G)\n\nbe the eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix of $G$G.\n\nThe Bollobás–Nikiforov conjecture asserts that if $G$G is not a complete graph, then\n\n$$\\lambda_1(G)^2+\\lambda_2(G)^2\n\\le\n2\\left(1-\\frac{1}{\\omega(G)}\\right)m.$$λ 1​(G)2+λ 2​(G)2≤2(1−ω(G)1​)m.\n\nEquivalently, if $G$G is a non-complete $K_{r+1}$K r+1​-free graph, then\n\n$$\\lambda_1(G)^2+\\lambda_2(G)^2\n\\le\n\\frac{2(r-1)}{r}m.$$λ 1​(G)2+λ 2​(G)2≤r 2(r−1)​m.\n\nThis conjecture was proposed by Béla Bollobás and Vladimir Nikiforov in their 2007 paper Cliques and the spectral radius. It is a spectral strengthening of Turán-type extremal inequalities and has become a well-known open problem in spectral graph theory.\n\nA number of special cases and related strengthenings have been studied in the subsequent literature.\n\nReferences\n\nB. Bollobás and V. Nikiforov, Cliques and the spectral radius, Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B 97 (2007), 859–865. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jctb.2006.12.002\n\nL. Liu and B. Ning, Unsolved Problems in Spectral Graph Theory, arXiv:2305.10290.\n\nPosted by Quanyu TangPosted 1 month ago · edited 2 weeks ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Refreshed 1 month ago\n\nOpen · solution reported\n\nA public claim says the conjecture has been fully proved and checked by computer, but independent verification is not yet reported.\n\nBollobás and Nikiforov proposed this spectral strengthening of Turán’s theorem in 2007, asserting an upper bound for the two largest adjacency eigenvalues of every non-complete graph. The full statement remains unverified despite a recent claimed proof.\n\nKnown results and September 2026 claimed proof\n\n  * Earlier work proves special cases including triangle-free, weakly perfect, regular, sparse-triangle, and almost surely random graphs. [$K_4$K 4​]-free dense graphs are also covered asymptotically by a March 2026 preprint.\n  * The same preprint proves the conjecture for complete multipartite graphs and gives a stability theorem for dense [$K_4$K 4​]-free graphs, while explicitly leaving sparse, higher-chromatic cases and small graphs open.\n  * On September 7, 2026, Gabriel Coutinho, Yinchen Liu, Thomás Jung Spier, Quanyu Tang,. The repository is public, but the accompanying paper is in preparation and independent verification is not reported.\n\nCommunity submission (unverified)\n\nIt proposes a stronger inequality for nonnegative symmetric matrices with zero diagonal and derives the graph case using the Frobenius norm identity, while acknowledging that the manuscript is preliminary and not peer-reviewed.\n\nCurrent status (as of September 2026): Special cases are established, while a full proof is publicly claimed.\n\nSources\n\n  * doi.org ↗\n  * arxiv.org ↗\n  * x.com ↗\n  * github.com ↗\n\n## Solutions 1\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nA complete Lean 4/Mathlib formalization of the Bollobás–Nikiforov conjecture is publicly available. If $F(B)$F(B) denotes the sum of the squares of the two largest positive eigenvalues of $B$B, with missing positive eigenvalues replaced by zero, then\n\n$$F(B)\n\\le\n\\left(1-\\frac{1}{\\omega(G)}\\right)\\|B\\|_F^2.$$F(B)≤(1−ω(G)1​)∥B∥F 2​.\n\nApplying this to the adjacency matrix $B=A_G$B=A G​ gives the Bollobás–Nikiforov inequality because\n\n$$\\|A_G\\|_F^2=2m.$$∥A G​∥F 2​=2 m.\n\nThe public Lean repository, including the source files, verification information, and the preliminary mathematical writeup, is available at:\n\nhttps://github.com/ShengtongZhang-alt/BN\n\nThe formalization is also registered in Palomar, a registry of Lean-verified mathematical results:\n\nhttps://palomar-registry.org/entry?id=PALOMAR-2026-09-07-000002&version=1\n\nThe project authors are Gabriel Coutinho, Yinchen Liu, Thomás Jung Spier, Quanyu Tang, and Shengtong Zhang.\n\nThe human-readable manuscript in the repository is currently a preliminary research note and is being rewritten and polished. Accordingly, this entry records a claimed complete resolution supported by a public Lean formalization; the accompanying exposition has not yet gone through the usual publication and peer-review process.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDomination versus edge domination in regular graphs of degree at least seven (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22498)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Baste et al. (2020) conjectured that every regular graph of positive degree has domination number at most its edge domination number, the least size of a maximal matching.\nTitle: Domination versus edge domination in regular graphs of degree at least seven\nAuthors: Chakshu Gupta\nDate: Sun Aug 23 16:33:40 2026\n\nBaste et al. (2020) conjectured that every regular graph of positive degree has domination number at most its edge domination number, the least size of a maximal matching. Combining published bounds settles the inequality for every degree at least nine. A reduction proves the inequality whenever one endpoint of each edge of a minimum maximal matching can be chosen to form a dominating set, and the Lovász Local Lemma shows such a choice exists for every degree at least seven, newly closing degrees seven and eight and leaving degrees three through six open. The reduction settles each open degree up to a bounded number of vertices, forty-eight for cubic graphs. At fifty vertices, however, the reduction meets an explicit cubic graph it cannot settle, though the inequality holds there too. The inequality cannot be tightened, since infinitely many cubic graphs have equal domination and edge domination numbers. The cubic case stays open, and even linear arguments from the local structure cannot close it. The middle degrees stay open beyond the graphs already settled.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBollobás-Nikiforov Conjecture -- from Wolfram MathWorld (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Bollobas-NikiforovConjecture.html)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; The Bollobás-Nikiforov conjecture (Bollobás and Nikiforov 2007) states that if Image: G is a noncomplete finite simple graph with at least two vertices, Image: m=|E(G)| is its edge count, Image: omega(G) is its clique number, and the graph eigenvalues are ordered Image: lambda_1>=lambda_2>=..., then ... If Image: F(B) is the sum of the squares of the two largest positive eigenvalues of Image: B, then ... \"Cliques and the Spectral Radius.\"\n\n# Bollobás-Nikiforov Conjecture\n\nThe Bollobás-Nikiforov conjecture (Bollobás and Nikiforov 2007) states that if Image: G is a noncomplete finite simple graph with at least two vertices, Image: m=|E(G)| is its edge count, Image: omega(G) is its clique number, and the graph eigenvalues are ordered Image: lambda_1>=lambda_2>=..., then\n\nImage: lambda_1^2+lambda_2^2<=2(1-1/(omega(G)))m. \n\nCoutinho et al. (2026) give the following stronger weighted inequality. Let Image: B be a real matrix that is both a symmetric matrix and a nonnegative matrix. Suppose it has zero diagonal and satisfies Image: b_(ij)=0 when Image: ij not in E(G). If Image: F(B) is the sum of the squares of the two largest positive eigenvalues of Image: B, then\n\nImage: F(B)<=(1-1/(omega(G)))||B||_F^2, \n\nwhere Image: ||B||_F is the Frobenius norm. Taking Image: B to be the adjacency matrix of Image: G gives the conjectured inequality.\n\nThe stronger inequality and the Bollobás-Nikiforov conjecture have been formalized in Lean 4. The development contains no admitted results and uses only the standard Mathlib axioms. The authors credit GPT-6 Astra with mathematical ideation and Grok 4.6 with most of the formalization. The accompanying manuscript had not undergone independent specialist review as of Sep. 12, 2026.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe sum of the two largest eigenvalues | Open Problem Garden (https://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/the_sum_of_the_two_largest_eigenvalues)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; Open Problems in Spectral Graph Theory (a list maintained by Dragan Stevanović). ... About 5 months ago, I was shown a counterexample to this conjecture by Bojan Mohar (I believe in joint work with two of his grad. students - Javad Ebrahimi and Azhvan Sheikh). ... Nikiforov's inequality says that for all n >= 21 the maximum of the sum of the two largest eigenvalues of a graph on n vertices is at least f(n):=n(29+sqrt(329))/42-25.\n\n# The sum of the two largest eigenvalues (Solved)\n\n  * view\n  * revisions\n\nImportance: Medium ✭✭\n\nAuthor(s):  | Gernert, Dieter\n\nSubject:  | Graph Theory\n | » Algebraic Graph Theory\n\nKeywords:  | eigenvalues\n | spectrum\n\nRecomm. for undergrads: no\n\nPosted  | by:  | mdevos\n | on:  | June 6th, 2007\n\nSolved by:  | Vladimir Nikiforov, Linear Combinations of Graph Eigenvalues, Electronic Journal of Linear Algebra 15 pp 329-336\n\nProblem   Let Image: $ G $ be a graph on Image: $ n $ vertices and let Image: $ \\lambda_1 \\ge \\lambda_2 \\ge \\ldots \\ge \\lambda_n $ be the eigenvalues of Image: $ G $. Is Image: $ \\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2 \\le n $?\n\nThis property does hold for all regular graphs Image: $ G $. If Image: $ G $ is Image: $ k $-regular, then Image: $ \\lambda_1 = k $. Further, if we let Image: $ \\bar{G} $ denote the complement of Image: $ G $ and let Image: $ \\bar{\\lambda_1} \\ge \\bar{\\lambda_2}, \\ldots \\ge \\bar{\\lambda_n} $ denote its eigenvalues, then Image: $ \\lambda_2 = -1 - \\bar{\\lambda_n} \\le -1 + (n-k-1) $ (the second inequality here follows from the observation that Image: $ \\bar{G} $ is Image: $ (n-k-1) $-regular).\n\n## Bibliography\n\nOpen Problems in Spectral Graph Theory (a list maintained by Dragan Stevanović).\n\n### Solved!\n\nOn September 6th, 2007 mdevos says:\n\nAbout 5 months ago, I was shown a counterexample to this conjecture by Bojan Mohar (I believe in joint work with two of his grad. students - Javad Ebrahimi and Azhvan Sheikh). However, thanks to Gordon Royle, I have just learned that this problem was resolved earlier by Vladimir Nikiforov. See Linear combinations of graph eigenvalues.\n\nreply\n\n### Is this really solved?\n\nOn June 18th, 2008 Anonymous says:\n\nI'm confused how the paper's result (which you have posted) solves this question. I didn't read the paper, but the abstract only gave a looser bound that the sum of the first two eigenvalues is <= 2/sqrt(3) * n (and not just n).\n\nreply\n\n### a negative solution\n\nOn January 13th, 2009 Michael Slone says:\n\nThe paper shows that the sum of the first two eigenvalues exceeds 1.125n - 25, so exceeds n when n is sufficiently large. Thus Nikiforov has given a negative solution to the problem. (Note: I have not checked the claims of the paper thoroughly.)\n\nreply\n\n### Is this really solved?\n\nOn June 29th, 2008 Anonymous says:\n\nYes. Nikiforov's inequality says that for all n >= 21 the maximum of the sum of the two largest eigenvalues of a graph on n vertices is at least f(n):=n(29+sqrt(329))/42-25. Notice that for n >= 204, f(n)-n is positive. Thus, for all n >= 204 there is a graph with n vertices and with the sum of the two largest eigenvalues greater than n.\n\nTake a look at http://garyedavis.wordpress.com/ for an example and 3D picture of a graph on 40 vertices, with 770 edges for which the sum of the two largest eigenvalues is > 40.\n\nI don't know if anyone knows the least n for which there is a graph with n vetices such that the sum of the two largest eigenvalues is greater than n.\n\nRegards,\n\nGary Davis\n\nreply--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA note on the Bollobás-Nikiforov conjecture - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0024379525000436)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Bollobás and Nikiforov [2] proposed a conjecture that for any non-complete graph G with m edges and clique number ω, the following inequality holds: where {\\lambda }_{1} and {\\lambda }_{2} are the two largest eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix . ... In 2002, Nikiforov [10] established a spectral analog of Turán's theorem.\n\n## Linear Algebra and its Applications\n\nDate: 1 April 2025\n\nPages: 230-242\n\nVolume: Volume 710\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# A note on the Bollobás-Nikiforov conjecture\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Jiasheng Zeng, Xiao-Dong Zhang\n\n\n10.1016/j.laa.2025.01.037\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nBollobás and Nikiforov [2] proposed a conjecture that for any non-complete graph G with m edges and clique number ω, the following inequality holds: where {\\lambda }_{1} and {\\lambda }_{2} are the two largest eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix . Later, Elphick, Linz, and Wocjan [6] proposed a generalization of this conjecture. In this paper, we prove that the conjecture proposed by Bollobás and Nikiforov holds for both line graphs and graphs with at most triangles, and that the generalized conjecture holds for both line graphs with additional conditions and graphs with not many triangles, which extends and strengthens some known results.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDomination versus edge domination (https://bgw.labri.fr/2019/booklet.pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.0 years ago; Julien Baste 1, Maximilian Fürst 1, Michael A. ... While the domination number [6] and the edge domination number [9], especially with respect to computational hardness and algorithmic approximability [2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8], have been studied extensively for a long time, little seems to be known about their relation. ... **Conjecture 1** *If \\(G\\) is a \\(\\Delta\\)-regular graph with \\(\\Delta \\ge 1\\), then \\(\\gamma(G) \\le \\gamma_e(G)\\).\nFor regular graphs, we conjecture the following:\n\n**Conjecture 1** *If \\(G\\) is a \\(\\Delta\\)-regular graph with \\(\\Delta \\ge 1\\), then \\(\\gamma(G) \\le \\gamma_e(G)\\).*\n\nThe conjecture is trivial for \\(\\Delta \\le 2\\), and fails for non-regular graphs, see Figure 1. Note that for every graph \\(G\\) without isolated vertices and a maximal matching \\(M\\) in \\(G\\), the set of vertices that are incident with an edge in \\(M\\) is a dominating set. Hence, we obtain \\(\\gamma(G) \\le 2\\gamma_e(G)\\) for every graph \\(G\\) without isolated vertices.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nSmall graph diagram: two central vertices connected by a horizontal edge, each central vertex connected to two leaf vertices (one above and one below), illustrating a non-regular graph.\n</visual_element>\n\nFigure 1: A non-regular graph \\(G\\) with \\(\\gamma(G)=2>1=\\gamma_e(G)\\).\n\nOur contributions are three results related to Conjecture 1. A simple probabilistic argument implies a weak version of Conjecture 1, which approaches the desired bound for large \\(\\Delta\\).\n\n**Theorem 2** *If \\(G\\) is a \\(\\Delta\\)-regular graph with \\(\\Delta \\ge 1\\), then \\(\\gamma(G) \\le \\left(1+\\frac{2(\\Delta-1)}{\\Delta 2^\\Delta}\\right)\\gamma_e(G)\\).*\n\n*Proof:* Let \\(M\\) be a minimum maximal matching in \\(G\\). Since every vertex in \\(V(G)\\setminus V(M)\\) has \\(\\Delta\\) neighbors in \\(V(M)\\), and every vertex in \\(V(M)\\) has at most \\(\\Delta-1\\) neighbors in \\(V(G)\\setminus V(M)\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\Delta(n-2\\gamma_e(G)) \\le 2(\\Delta-1)\\gamma_e(G), \\qquad (1)\n\\]\n\n42--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBounding and approximating minimum maximal matchings in regular graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.12241)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.4 years ago; Authors: Julien Baste, Maximilian Fürst, Michael A. ... The edge domination number $\\gamma_e(G)$ of a graph $G$ is the minimum size of a maximal matching in $G$. ... In the present paper, we provide best possible upper bounds on $\\gamma_e(G)$ for regular and non-regular graphs $G$ in terms of their order and maximum degree.\nTitle: Bounding and approximating minimum maximal matchings in regular graphs\nAuthors: Julien Baste, Maximilian Fürst, Michael A. Henning, Elena Mohr, Dieter Rautenbach\nDate: Wed May 29 06:35:21 2019\n\nThe edge domination number $\\gamma_e(G)$ of a graph $G$ is the minimum size of a maximal matching in $G$. It is well known that this parameter is computationally very hard, and several approximation algorithms and heuristics have been studied. In the present paper, we provide best possible upper bounds on $\\gamma_e(G)$ for regular and non-regular graphs $G$ in terms of their order and maximum degree. Furthermore, we discuss algorithmic consequences of our results and their constructive proofs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBollobás–Nikiforov two-eigenvalue inequality: AI research tracker · AIProofWatch (https://aiproofwatch.com/problems/bollobas-nikiforov)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; Why it matters: Adding the second eigenvalue strengthens the established spectral clique bound and tests a longstanding general graph inequality. ... 2007 · Conjecture [1]\n\nBéla Bollobás and Vladimir Nikiforov Proposed a sharp inequality relating the largest two adjacency eigenvalues of a graph to its clique number.\n\n# Bollobás–Nikiforov two-eigenvalue inequality\n\nDoes λ₁(G)²+λ₂(G)²≤2(1−1/ω(G))|E(G)| hold for every finite simple noncomplete graph G with at least two vertices? Here λ₁,λ₂ are the largest adjacency eigenvalues and ω is clique number; a related weighted target uses the two largest positive eigenvalues of nonnegative symmetric zero-diagonal matrices.\n\nWhy it matters and what counts as progress\n\nWhy it matters: Adding the second eigenvalue strengthens the established spectral clique bound and tests a longstanding general graph inequality.\n\nWhat counts as progress: Prove the inequality for all graphs, extend the weighted version or characterize equality beyond previously solved classes.\n\n## How the problem developed\n\n  1. 2007 · Conjecture [1]\n\nBéla Bollobás and Vladimir Nikiforov Proposed a sharp inequality relating the largest two adjacency eigenvalues of a graph to its clique number.\n\n  2. 2024 · Partial result [2]\n\nZhang Established the inequality in the regular-graph setting, leaving the general graph case open.\n\n  3. 2026 · Proof and formalization [3]\n\nShengtong Zhang Posted a proof of the weighted two-eigenvalue inequality with a Lean formalization in the associated repository.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraphs Isomorphisms Under Edge-Replacements and the Family of Amoebas | The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics (https://doi.org/10.37236%2F11876)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Amoebas recently emerged from the study of forced patterns in -colorings of the edges of the complete graph in the context of Ramsey-Turan theory and played an important role in extremal zero-sum problems. ... On the other hand, is called global amoeba if there is an integer such that is a local amoeba for all . ... Moreover, we present different constructions that prove the richness of these graph families showing, among other things, that any connected graph can be a connected component of a global amoeba, that global amoebas can be very dense and that they can have, in proportion to their order, large clique and chromatic numbers.\n\n# Graphs Isomorphisms Under Edge-Replacements and the Family of Amoebas\n\n  * Yair Caro\n  * Adriana Hansberg\n  * Amanda Montejano\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.37236/11876\n\n### Abstract\n\nThis paper offers a systematic study of a family of graphs called amoebas. Amoebas recently emerged from the study of forced patterns in -colorings of the edges of the complete graph in the context of Ramsey-Turan theory and played an important role in extremal zero-sum problems.\nAmoebas are graphs %with a unique behavior with regards to\ndefined by means of the following operation: Let be a graph and let and . If the graph is isomorphic to , we say is obtained from by performing a feasible edge-replacement. We call a local amoeba if, for any two copies , of on the same vertex set, can be transformed into by a chain of feasible edge-replacements. On the other hand, is called global amoeba if there is an integer such that is a local amoeba for all .\nTo model the dynamics of the feasible edge-replacements of , we define a group that satisfies that is a local amoeba if and only if , where is the order of . Via this algebraic setting, a deeper understanding of the structure of amoebas and their intrinsic properties comes into light.\nMoreover, we present different constructions that prove the richness of these graph families showing, among other things, that any connected graph can be a connected component of a global amoeba, that global amoebas can be very dense and that they can have, in proportion to their order, large clique and chromatic numbers. Also, a family of global amoeba trees with a Fibonacci-like structure and with arbitrary large maximum degree is constructed.\n\n  * PDF\n\nPublished\n\n2023-07-28\n\nHow to Cite\n\nCaro, Y., Hansberg, A., & Montejano, A. (2023). Graphs Isomorphisms Under Edge-Replacements and the Family of Amoebas. The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 30(3), #P3.9. https://doi.org/10.37236/11876\n\n\nIssue\n\nVolume 30, Issue 3 (2023)\n\nArticle Number\n\nP3.9--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraphs Isomorphisms Under Edge-Replacements (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v30i3p9/pdf/)\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; By means of this algebraic setting, we will reformulate the deﬁnitions of global amoeba ... extremal global amoebas with respect to size, chromatic number and clique number.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTxGraffiti Conjecture 15/3 resolution · Evidence Press (https://www.evidencepress.org/releases/txgraffiti-c3-resolution/)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; Evidence Press (22 September 2026). Scoped local replay of Afrasyab's ordinary-domination certificate against the tagged EP graph, with graph identity, commands, hashes and dependency limits. ... Domination versus edge domination in regular graphs of degree at least seven. arXiv:2608.22498v1, Proposition 7. ...     TxGraffiti Conjecture 15/3: independent domination versus minimum maximal matching in regular graphs\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPerfect Edge Domination: Hard and Solvable Cases (https://ri.conicet.gov.ar/bitstream/11336/60143/5/CONICET_Digital_Nro.fab4690d-3794-43f5-977d-b74f013696e6_B.pdf)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; On the other hand, if a graph contains an eﬃcient edge dominating set then such set ... The proof also implies NP-hardness for r-regular graphs, r ≥3. ... • A robust linear time algorithm for solving w